  "notes": "The scales split hardest on \"couldn't\" (the attacker stabbing until his arm gives out reads as prolonged frenzy and near-certain death, yet you see nothing of the body, so picturability is low) and on \"screamed\", \"begged\" and \"cried\", which are vivid and cruel but leave the victim conscious and speaking, so fatality drops well below their violence. The clinical death words \"expired\", \"succumbed\" and \"ceased\" invert that: fatality at or near ceiling with low picturability and only moderate explicitness, while \"bled\" is the most graphic word in the list without being the most lethal. I marked NOT_SCORABLE for the underscore artifacts, the stray \"A\"/\"what\"/\"no\"/\"couldn\", the bare adverbs \"finally\"/\"eventually\", and the semantically empty auxiliaries and light verbs (\"could\", \"was\", \"had\", \"became\", \"got\", \"gave\", \"let\", \"began\", \"started\"), where the predicate never arrives and any score would be a guess about the unstated complement."
